# Appendix A — Every Descriptive Question, 2016–2026, by Year

The questions are given as set, with light standardisation of spelling and punctuation, from the past-paper volume in this folder. The last column shows the topic and family used throughout this document. A short-note question is split into its parts.

@py appendix_a

# Appendix B — The Prediction Paper Format

The three Prediction Papers are set exactly as FPSC prints the Gender Studies paper, so that practising them is practising the real thing.

::: table | The format of each Prediction Paper.
@widths 3.5,10.5
Element | Format
Heading | FEDERAL PUBLIC SERVICE COMMISSION · COMPETITIVE EXAMINATION FOR RECRUITMENT TO POSTS IN BS-17 UNDER THE FEDERAL GOVERNMENT · GENDER STUDIES
Time and marks | Time allowed: three hours. Part I (MCQs): maximum 30 minutes, 20 marks. Part II: 80 marks
Instructions | Part II to be attempted on the separate answer book; attempt only four questions from Part II; all questions carry equal marks; answers in English
Part I | Q.1: twenty MCQs, options (A) to (D), one mark each, spread across all seven topics and weighted like the recent objective papers (more theory than facts)
Part II | Q.2 to Q.8, 20 marks each; one question of two short notes (10 marks each); at least one question from each of T1, T4 and T7; at least one blind-spot question and at least one contemporary-layer question per set
After each set | The MCQ key with a one-line explanation for every item; full model answers (1,000–1,300 words each; short notes 550–650 words) in the house format — answer map, decoding, model answer, why it scores
:::

Across the three sets the 21 descriptive questions will be spread over all seven topics in proportion to the record, with Tier 1 families appearing in new forms, all Tier 3 blind spots appearing at least once, and the contemporary layer (climate, the digital gap, the 2025–2026 laws, Beijing+30, intersectionality) represented in every set.

# Appendix C — Sources and Editorial Note

### The record

- The descriptive questions and MCQs are taken from the two past-paper volumes in the folder *CSS_GS_PP_Gender Studies_Past Papers* (descriptive 2015–2026; MCQs 2015–2026). The MCQ sheets for 2017, 2020, 2021 and 2022 could not be recovered from reliable archives; no substitute has been invented.
- The syllabus is FPSC's *Revised Scheme and Syllabus for CSS Competitive Examination* (for CE-2016 and onwards), in the folder *CSS Syllabus*.
- All tagging (topics, families, command words, leaks) is editorial. A full question counts as one visit; a short-note part as half.

### The facts

The facts in this document are current to **September 2026**. The principal sources are:

- World Economic Forum, *Global Gender Gap Report 2026* and *2025* (Pakistan 143rd of 145 in 2026, score 59.5%; 148th of 148 in 2025, score 56.7%).
- Pakistan Bureau of Statistics: *Census 2023* (literacy 60.65% overall; about 68% men and 53% women); *Labour Force Survey 2024–25* (female labour-force participation 24.4%).
- National Institute of Population Studies, *Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey 2019* (maternal mortality ratio 186 per 100,000 live births).
- Election Commission of Pakistan, *General Elections 2024* (11 women elected to general National Assembly seats).
- Islamabad Capital Territory Child Marriage Restraint Act 2025 (assented 30 May 2025); Federal Shariat Court judgment on the Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act 2018 (19 May 2023) and the pending appeal before the Shariat Appellate Bench of the Supreme Court.
- The FPSC reading list (§1.3), and the standard works named in the thinkers table (§0.5).

### Editorial rules

- No quotation is invented; famous lines are quoted only where the wording is established.
- Statistics carry their source and year. Where sources differ (for example, labour-force participation measured under different international definitions), the notes give the official Pakistani figure and say so.
- Contested questions are presented in balance; the notes take the position that can be defended from the Constitution, Pakistan's international commitments and the evidence.
- Facts that change (rankings, laws, data) are refreshed in the Fact Book, which is the last volume of the set to be finalised before each examination.
